<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Nutcracker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Shchelkunchik, Щелкунчик</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nutcracker is a two-act classical ballet by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky</a>, first performed on 18 December 1892 at the Imperial Mariinsky <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in a double premiere with his last opera, Iolanta. Its plot, set on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Eve, adapts Alexandre Dumas's 1844 retelling of E. T. A. Hoffmann's 1816 story The Nutcracker and the Mouse King. The libretto was by Marius Petipa; whether he or his assistant Lev Ivanov choreographed the first production has been debated. The first performance was not deemed a success. Since the late 1960s the ballet has been danced by many companies, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>; its score is noted for its use of the celesta.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-nutcracker.jpg" alt="The Nutcracker" width="330" height="273" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous Russian 19th century photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nutcracker1892.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
